@page "/docs/get-started/create-app"
@inject ApiMetadata Api

@* A new app from the blazor starter (catalog/blazor, published as IzzyBlazor.Templates). The
   options are the starter's, as izzy describe starter blazor lists them. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="create-app">
    <Lead>
        IzzyBlazor's starter is Microsoft's Blazor Web App, rebuilt with IzzyBlazor. Its layout, pages and account pages
        use IzzyBlazor's components and theme, in every render mode. It accepts the same options as
        <Code>dotnet new blazor</Code>.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <TextBlock>
            For an app you already have, see <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app">Add to an existing app</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Heading Level="2">Install the templates</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The starter is a <Code>dotnet new</Code> template, like Microsoft's own <Code>blazor</Code> template. Microsoft's
            templates come with the .NET SDK. IzzyBlazor's come in a NuGet package, so you install them once. With the
            .NET 10 SDK, run:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@Install</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The command includes a version because IzzyBlazor is in preview. Without one, <Code>dotnet new install</Code>
            looks for a stable release.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Create the app</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@Create</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Open the URL that <Code>dotnet run</Code> prints. The app has a sidebar, a home page, and sample Counter, Weather
            and Contact pages. Its pages are <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">static</Anchor> by default. A page runs
            on the server over a circuit once you give it a render mode, and Counter already has one.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Choose the render modes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>The render mode options are Microsoft's, with the same names and defaults:</TextBlock>
        <Table Items="RenderModes" Caption="Render mode options" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Option" Width="Length.Rem(17)"><code>@context!.Option</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What you get">@context!.Result</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            With WebAssembly or Auto, the app is two projects: <Code>MyApp</Code> on the server and <Code>MyApp.Client</Code>
            for the components that run in the browser. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes#pages-in-the-browser">Pages in the browser</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Whichever you choose, a page you don't mark stays static, so the choice is what a page can become, not what
            every page is. <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/what-each-render-mode-adds">What each render mode adds</Anchor>
            counts what a page needs in each.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Accounts</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>--auth Individual</Code> adds login, registration and account management with ASP.NET Core Identity.
            Accounts are stored in a SQLite database. To use SQL Server LocalDB instead, add <Code>--use-local-db</Code>.
            The account pages keep Microsoft's code and use IzzyBlazor's components, so Microsoft's fixes still apply to them.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Theme and layout</Heading>
        <TextBlock>IzzyBlazor's own options set the theme and the shell around every page:</TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Looks" Caption="Theme and layout options" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Option" Width="Length.Rem(17)"><code>@context!.Option</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Result</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>To create a dense admin app with links across the top, run:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet new starter-blazor -n Admin --preset Compact --brand teal --nav Top</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To see the other options, such as <Code>--no-https</Code> and <Code>--use-program-main</Code>, run
            <Code>dotnet new starter-blazor --help</Code>. To change the theme later, set it on <Code>IzzyTheme</Code>. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/theming/themes">Themes and presets</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">With the izzy CLI</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy new</Code> creates the same app and accepts the same options. To add
            <Anchor Href="blocks">blocks</Anchor> to the app afterwards, install the CLI. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/cli/overview">The izzy CLI</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy new starter blazor -n MyApp</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To start from a working app instead, such as a CRM, see <Anchor Href="templates">Templates</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/cli/overview">The izzy CLI</Anchor>: add a hero, pricing or a sign-up form to the app as code you own.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: every component, with what it does in each render mode.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/get-started/ai-assistants">AI assistants</Anchor>: <Code>--mcp</Code> connects your AI assistant to IzzyBlazor's docs. With <Code>izzy new starter blazor</Code>, it also adds the skill and the izzy server.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Choice(string Option, string Result);

    private string Install => $"dotnet new install IzzyBlazor.Templates@{Api.Version}";

    private const string Create = """
        dotnet new starter-blazor -n MyApp
        cd MyApp
        dotnet run
        """;

    private static readonly Choice[] RenderModes =
    [
        new("--interactivity Server", "The default. Pages are static, and a page you mark runs on the server over a circuit."),
        new("--interactivity None", "Every page is static. The app never opens a circuit."),
        new("--interactivity WebAssembly", "A page you mark runs in the browser, from a .Client project."),
        new("--interactivity Auto", "A page you mark runs on the server at first, and in the browser once WebAssembly has loaded."),
        new("--all-interactive", "Every page is interactive in the chosen render mode, except pages marked to stay static, such as the account pages."),
    ];

    private static readonly Choice[] Looks =
    [
        new("--preset <name>", "Sets the theme: Standard, Compact for data and admin apps, Soft for consumer apps, or Editorial for reading and docs."),
        new("--brand <color>", "Sets the brand color: blue, ink, indigo, violet, teal, green or rose, or a hex color such as #0a7d5a."),
        new("--nav Top", "Puts the navigation in the top bar instead of a sidebar. On phones, it folds into a menu."),
        new("--shell <look>", "Sets how the page stands apart from the shell: Tinted (the default), Inset (a raised panel), Flat (lines only) or Canvas (a tinted page for cards)."),
        new("--content-width <rem>", "Sets how wide pages get: Full, or 64, 72 or 80 rem. Auto, the default, fills the space beside a sidebar, and centers a 72 rem page under a top bar."),
        new("--empty", "Leaves out the sample pages and the sidebar."),
        new("--mcp", "Connects Claude Code, VS Code, Visual Studio and Cursor to IzzyBlazor's docs server."),
    ];
}
